1. Requirements

a. Functional Requirements
    Below are the requirements and their description.
      i. Authentication - The user can register and login account in software.
      i. Create a Social Media Post - Users can create / edit / delete social media post. A social media post can be Text post, Image post or a Video post. 
      ii. Like / Unlike Posts - Users can like / Unlike other people's post.
      iii. Follow / Unfollow - Users can follow / unfollow users.
      iv. Comment on Posts - Users can comment on each other posts.
      v. News Feed - Users can view news feed. The feed shows posts from people they follow, listed from newest to oldest.
      vi. Like / Unlike Graph - Users can users like / unlike graph in dashboard.
      vii. Followers Graph - Users can see followers Increasing / Decreasing graph in dashboard.
      viii. Engagement Graph - Users can see comments / engagements Graph (comments directly proportional to engagements)

  b. Non-Functional Requirements
      
      i. Eventual Consistency - If the user posts something, it should show up within 2 seconds on News Feed.
      ii. Like / Unlike Update - If the user like / unlike on post, it should update like / unlike graph in dashboard within 2 seconds.
      iii. Follow / Unfollow Update - If the user follows a user, it should update follow / unfollow graph in dashboard within 2 seconds.
      iv. Engagement Update - If a user comments on the post, it should update users engagement graph in dashboard within 2 seconds.
      v. Usability - The rendering should be fast. Example: It's frustrating if a post loads in parts (like the image first, then text, etc.). The post should appear almost instantly.
      vi. Load Latency - The News Feed should load within 1-2 seconds

  c. Architectue Requirements
      
      i. Extensibility - The design of our system should be such that it is easier to extend it in the future. Example: If we need to add features like replying to a comment, post recommendations, or ads.
      ii. Modularity - The system should split in small independent "modules".
      iii. Cohesion and Coupling - The system should have "high cohesion" and "low coupling".
      iv. Microservice - The system should use small independent services, each one runs on its own, focuses on one job.
      

